                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 70-year-old female patient, admitted on 27/07/1989 with acute lower abdominal pain and suspected ectopic pregnancy. On admission, clinical examination and transvaginal ultrasonography confirmed a tubal ectopic pregnancy with signs of early rupture. Serum beta-hCG was markedly elevated. Given the patient's hemodynamic status and imaging findings, the decision was made for surgical intervention. On 27/07/1989, laparoscopic salpingectomy was performed without complications. Histopathological examination confirmed the diagnosis of tubal ectopic pregnancy. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Vital signs remained stable throughout the stay. The patient was mobilized early and discharged home on 29/07/1989 in good general condition. Please ensure follow-up including monitoring of beta-hCG levels to confirm complete resolution. I remain available for any further questions.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
